{title: O for a Thousand Tongues to Sing}
{lyricist: Charles Wesley}
{composer: Carl G. Gläser}
{meta: arranger Lowell Mason}
{year: 1739}
{meta: tune-year 1839}
{key: G}
{meta: tune AZMON}
{meta: meter CM (8.6.8.6)}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to AZMON}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags praise, Methodist}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
O [G]for a thousand [D]tongues to [G]sing
My [G]great Re[Am]deemer's [D]praise,
The [D7]glories [G]of my [C]God and [G]King,
The [G]triumphs [D7]of His [G]grace!
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
My [G]gracious Master [D]and my [G]God,
As[G]sist me [Am]to pro[D]claim,
To [D7]spread through [G]all the [C]earth a[G]broad
The [G]honors [D7]of Thy [G]name.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Je[G]sus! the name that [D]charms our [G]fears,
That [G]bids our [Am]sorrows [D]cease;
'Tis [D7]music [G]in the [C]sinner's [G]ears,
'Tis [G]life, and [D7]health, and [G]peace.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
He [G]breaks the power of [D]canceled [G]sin,
He [G]sets the [Am]prisoner [D]free;
His [D7]blood can [G]make the [C]foulest [G]clean;
His [G]blood a[D7]vailed for [G]me.
{end_of_verse}
